[Music]  staying at home during a lockdown  with much time to eat and little  movement at the same time  no wonder that many people all over the  world have gained weight during the  pandemic  and while it is quite a stretch from too  much comfort eating to straightforward  gluttony  too much body fat is bad for our health  in fact those additional pounds can  increase our risk of severe illness from  [Music]  covid19  i am overweight and i think i need to  lose  a bit of weight so that in case i get  infected  i'm not in in the danger zone  excess weight has always been a health  risk in times of the coronavirus  pandemic  it could be lethal  [Music]  welcome to our curvy 19 special on dw  news i'm monica jones in berlin good to  have you with us  now then when it rains it pours it's the  perfect idiom for overweight and obese  people these days  they already have enough health worries  as it is  and now a tiny virus is adding to their  plight  putting them into the same risk category  as the elderly and especially vulnerable  dr marcus reiser heads the covid19 unit  here in clinicum fest a hospital  in western germany most of the serious  cases they've seen here during the  pandemic are elderly patients  as well as another high-risk group what  we've seen on our covet unit is that  patients who are overweight  especially those who are morbidly obese  have worse outcomes in this infection  they're more likely to need intensive  care  studies in several countries have shown  a link between excess weight and severe  covid19 outcomes sylvia schaefer  is overweight and she survived the  covid19 infection in early march  first she developed a sudden fever and a  dry cough  a few days later she tested positive  i was in the hospital and they told me i  was a risk patient and that it could end  fatally  two years earlier she had been diagnosed  with diabetes  to get the disease under control she  changed her diet and lost a lot of  weight  she's convinced that helped her to get  through the covid19 infection  relatively easily if covid19 had  appeared a year earlier than it did  i don't think it would have ended that  well for me  with poorly managed diabetes and high  blood pressure  and much more excess weight than now 35  kilograms  i think that would have finished me off  obesity has become a global phenomenon  it's usually expressed using the body  mass index  or bmi which is the ratio of a person's  body weight in kilograms  to the square of their height in meters  a bmi of 20 to 25 is considered a  healthy weight  25 to 30 overweight 30 to 40  is classified as obese with over 40  viewed as severely obese  we don't just have a covet pandemic we  have an obesity pandemic all around the  world and in germany too  at clinic specialized obesity center  martin busing is a bariatric surgeon he  can hardly keep up with the many obese  people seeking  gastric bypass surgery and other weight  loss procedures  in germany their numbers are climbing at  the moment more than 2 million people in  the country  would qualify for this kind of surgery  with a bmi of over  40.  severe obesity goes hand in hand with a  number of other conditions  including diabetes respiratory problems  cardiovascular diseases  dr boozing puts this development mainly  down to poor dietary habits and over  consumption of sugary drinks  what i see again and again in our  overweight patients  is that they simply can't drink plain  unflavored water  soda water or tap water they always need  some kind of sweetener in it  it's developed into a kind of addiction  better prevention can help but when it  comes to that  health systems often fail and the food  industry has a powerful interest in the  nutritional agenda  both these problems explain why the  doctors here continue to battle two  pandemics  covet 19 and obesity  now for more i'm joined by susan jepp  nutrition scientist and professor of  diet and population health at the  university of oxford  good to have you with us let me start  straight away  with the way our report ended it  mentions there that  we're battling two pandemics obesity  and covet 19. would you agree  i absolutely would i mean in the uk  we're facing the incredibly sad  situation that we've had over 70  000 deaths from covert um that's  awful it attracts headlines every single  day  but the fact is that around 60 000  people's deaths are attributable to  obesity  every single year this is uh obesity is  a problem which has been with us for  decades  and shows no sign of going away there is  no vaccine for obesity  right so it has been a big problem  even before the pandemic however now  with the pandemic it seems to be  exacerbated and you've conducted a study  involving seven million people  looking at the connection between  obesity and kobe 19.  what are your findings  well some colleagues have a fantastic  data set  which um draws on data from uh primary  care  health records so electronic health  records so it's all anonymized  but we have so we don't know who these  people are but we do have data  about their weight and about whether  they have had a positive covered test  whether they've been admitted to  hospital and so forth  what we were able to look at was the  relationship between  people's bmi their body mass index a  measure of their weight status  and the likelihood of going into  hospital or being admitted to intensive  care  or dying from covert and what we saw was  that there was a striking association  with these if you like severe outcomes  from covid  in particular admission to intensive  care  so for people with a body mass index of  30 the level that we say  people have clinical obesity actually  your chances of going into intensive  care  are about three times greater than  somebody with a healthy weight  by the time you're 40 it's over six  times higher but the problem the problem  is to a large extent that a lot of  people are not aware  uh uh certainly not aware of their bmi  or that they  are are viewed as adoptees and are  at such risk  well many people might not consider  themselves to perhaps be living with  obesity  but i think many people are aware that  they're a bit heavier than perhaps  is good for their health um the problem  is that we've struggled  to convert that sort of you know general  understanding that awaits a bit of a  problem into a real sense of the need  to act and what perhaps covid has  started to do  is make us really recognize that obesity  isn't just about how we look actually  the really important issue  is the effect it has on our health and  obesity  affects every single organ system of the  body so  we know it leads to more diabetes more  heart disease  many different cancers are increased  people have  problems with their joints there's a  whole raft of ill health that goes with  obesity  so it's perhaps not so very surprising  that this extra  strain that being overweight puts on  your body  also increases your risk from covert now  of course we hear all the time that uh  we have to protect the elderly the  vulnerable but nobody ever talks about  protecting the uh overweight the obese  what can we do about that well we need  to recognize that being severely  overweight  creates real vulnerability and so i  think we need to kind of add these to  our list of people  who need extra take extra care during  the pandemic  all right susan jepp the nutrition  scientist and professor of diet and  population health at the university of  oxford thank you so much  thank you and before you step on your  scale at home now to check if  you've gained a few pounds too many  let's bring in another aspect of this  pandemic  science and its ability to solve  problems  time for your questions and over to our  science correspondent  derek williams  if covet vaccines work could the  research help developers create vaccines  for the common cold  could kova 19 lead to a huge step  forward in this area  i love questions like this because they  don't involve  where we are now which in europe at  least often feels like a pretty dire  place  but instead they look forward to the end  of the pandemic and they ask  whether all the misery might lead to  anything  positive but but while it's possible  that the vast numbers of research  projects being driven by covid19  could lead to breakthroughs in fighting  coronaviruses  as a whole there's still a lot we don't  understand about  how they in general evolve and and how  they're transmitted um  what's called cross-reactivity would  certainly  play a central role in research into any  future  offshoot of vaccines and that's when  the weapons that your immune system  produces to fight one virus  might also work at least to some extent  against other viruses that share  similar characteristics um some experts  believe that cross reactivity from  earlier infections with cold causing  coronaviruses  in some people is influencing how badly  they get covet 19 or  or how easily they fight off the disease  but we still don't know nearly enough  about it one promising avenue for  example that could help  in the development of future coronavirus  vaccines  involves a molecular subunits  in in the spikes those those proteins  that stick out of their surfaces  that allow these viruses to to latch  onto cells  and infect them um researchers have  discovered that some of those  areas look uh well conserved  in all of the coronaviruses that infect  humans um they're  so they're quite similar across the  group um that's a great  starting point for future work but but  it's still just a starting point  so so when you ask whether covid19  could contribute in a big way to  coronavirus research  in general i'd say it already has  but i also don't think that'll  necessarily  be enough to spawn vaccines  in the near term for the coronaviruses  that cause  colds though i'd be happy if that  prediction  proved wrong  you

